WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:03.724
On the twenty-sixth of December, two thousand four, the sea rose

00:03.724 --> 00:06.788
over Marina Beach with no storm and no warning.

00:06.788 --> 00:10.375
The cause was far across the Bay of Bengal, under the ocean floor

00:10.375 --> 00:14.343
near Sumatra. On the seventeenth of January, nineteen ninety-five,

00:14.343 --> 00:17.720
an earthquake in Kobe, Japan, brought down elevated highways,

00:17.720 --> 00:19.463
and caused no tsunami at all.

00:19.463 --> 00:21.406
Both were faults releasing energy.

00:21.406 --> 00:23.880
This video explains why the results were so different.

00:23.880 --> 00:27.640
Plates move all the time, but the faults between them do not.

00:27.640 --> 00:30.756
A fault is a break in the rock, and friction keeps it locked.

00:30.756 --> 00:34.777
So the rock on either side bends, very slowly, and stores energy

00:34.777 --> 00:38.226
like a bent ruler. This can go on for decades or centuries.

00:38.226 --> 00:40.411
Then the fault can no longer hold.

00:40.411 --> 00:43.873
It slips, the rock snaps back toward its original shape, and the

00:43.873 --> 00:47.570
stored energy is released in seconds as waves that shake the ground.

00:47.570 --> 00:48.735
That is an earthquake.

00:48.735 --> 00:51.702
Then the fault locks again, and the cycle starts over.

00:51.702 --> 00:55.160
Magnitude measures the energy an earthquake releases.

00:55.160 --> 00:56.979
The scale is not like a ruler.

00:56.979 --> 01:00.573
Each whole number is about thirty-two times more energy than the

01:00.573 --> 01:04.257
one before. So a magnitude seven releases about thirty-two times

01:04.257 --> 01:08.008
the energy of a six, and a magnitude nine releases about a thousand

01:08.008 --> 01:09.532
times the energy of a seven.

01:09.532 --> 01:14.006
What sets the size is mainly the area of the fault that slips,

01:14.006 --> 01:15.652
and how far it slips.

01:15.652 --> 01:17.914
A bigger area means a bigger earthquake.

01:17.914 --> 01:20.802
Keep that in mind, because it explains the next slide.

01:20.802 --> 01:24.830
At a subduction zone, the contact between the two plates is a

01:24.830 --> 01:26.222
huge, sloping surface.

01:26.222 --> 01:28.387
The shallow part of it is locked.

01:28.387 --> 01:31.679
For centuries, the edge of the upper plate is dragged down and

01:31.679 --> 01:35.317
squeezed. When the locked zone finally breaks, that edge springs

01:35.317 --> 01:39.188
back up, and the seafloor rises by several meters in a few minutes.

01:39.188 --> 01:41.885
The whole column of water above it is lifted.

01:41.885 --> 01:45.417
That is a tsunami. It spreads out across the ocean at the speed

01:45.417 --> 01:48.693
of a jet aircraft. In deep water it is less than a meter high,

01:48.693 --> 01:50.366
and a ship would not notice it.

01:50.366 --> 01:52.843
Near the shore it slows down and piles up.

01:52.843 --> 01:54.914
Here are two subduction earthquakes.

01:54.914 --> 01:58.609
In two thousand four, about one thousand three hundred kilometers

01:58.609 --> 02:02.359
of the plate boundary off Sumatra and the Andaman Islands slipped.

02:02.359 --> 02:04.263
The magnitude was nine point one.

02:04.263 --> 02:07.733
The tsunami crossed the whole Indian Ocean, and more than two

02:07.733 --> 02:11.484
hundred and twenty thousand people died in over a dozen countries,

02:11.484 --> 02:15.011
including India. In two thousand eleven, the Tohoku earthquake

02:15.011 --> 02:16.746
struck off the coast of Japan.

02:16.746 --> 02:18.425
Its magnitude was about nine.

02:18.425 --> 02:22.176
The tsunami was more than ten meters high along much of the coast,

02:22.176 --> 02:25.479
and it went over sea walls that had been built to stop it.

02:25.479 --> 02:29.062
Nearly twenty thousand people died, in one of the best prepared

02:29.062 --> 02:30.405
countries in the world.

02:30.405 --> 02:32.593
Now compare a fault that slides sideways.

02:32.593 --> 02:36.437
This is what happens at a transform boundary, and on many smaller

02:36.437 --> 02:38.864
faults too. The two sides move horizontally.

02:38.864 --> 02:42.405
The seafloor is not lifted, so there is usually no tsunami.

02:42.405 --> 02:45.710
The area of the fault is smaller, so the magnitude is smaller

02:45.710 --> 02:49.114
as well. But these earthquakes are shallow, and the faults often

02:49.114 --> 02:50.746
run under or beside cities.

02:50.746 --> 02:54.416
In nineteen ninety-five, a magnitude six point nine earthquake

02:54.416 --> 02:58.047
struck Kobe, on a fault directly beneath a city of one and a half

02:58.047 --> 03:01.248
million people. The shaking lasted about twenty seconds.

03:01.248 --> 03:03.353
More than six thousand people died.

03:03.353 --> 03:07.119
It released far less energy than the Sumatra earthquake, and it

03:07.119 --> 03:10.442
was still devastating, because it was shallow and directly under

03:10.442 --> 03:13.878
a city. So magnitude alone does not decide the damage.

03:13.878 --> 03:15.955
Four things do. The first is size.

03:15.955 --> 03:17.973
The second is depth and distance.

03:17.973 --> 03:21.560
A shallow earthquake close to a city is far worse than a deep

03:21.560 --> 03:23.965
one far away. The third is the ground.

03:23.965 --> 03:27.692
Soft, wet ground shakes harder than solid rock, and it can briefly

03:27.692 --> 03:28.946
behave like a liquid.

03:28.946 --> 03:30.666
That is called liquefaction.

03:30.666 --> 03:32.186
The fourth is buildings.

03:32.186 --> 03:35.884
Most people who die in earthquakes die in buildings that collapse.

03:35.884 --> 03:39.596
In two thousand one, a magnitude seven point seven earthquake

03:39.596 --> 03:42.829
struck Bhuj, in Gujarat, far from any plate boundary.

03:42.829 --> 03:46.725
More than thirteen thousand people died, most of them in buildings

03:46.725 --> 03:48.807
that were not built to survive shaking.

03:48.807 --> 03:50.075
Can people be warned?

03:50.075 --> 03:52.647
Earthquakes cannot be predicted to the day.

03:52.647 --> 03:55.966
Scientists can say where they are likely to happen and how large

03:55.966 --> 03:59.037
they could be, and that is what building rules are based on.

03:59.037 --> 04:00.353
Tsunamis are different.

04:00.353 --> 04:03.921
The wave takes time to cross an ocean, so a warning is possible.

04:03.921 --> 04:07.190
In two thousand four there was no warning system in the Indian

04:07.190 --> 04:11.287
Ocean. India's tsunami warning center opened in two thousand seven.

04:11.287 --> 04:13.207
There is also a natural warning.

04:13.207 --> 04:16.407
If you are at the coast and you feel strong shaking, or shaking

04:16.407 --> 04:19.406
that goes on for a long time, or you see the sea suddenly draw

04:19.406 --> 04:21.567
back, go to high ground at once.

04:21.567 --> 04:22.848
Do not wait to be told.

04:22.848 --> 04:25.168
Check yourself with three questions.

04:25.168 --> 04:26.898
Pause if you need to.

04:26.898 --> 04:30.752
One. A subduction earthquake can make a tsunami, and a fault that

04:30.752 --> 04:33.682
slides sideways usually cannot.

04:33.682 --> 04:37.241
Why? Two. How much more energy does a magnitude eight release

04:37.241 --> 04:39.288
than a magnitude six?

04:39.288 --> 04:43.887
Three. You are at the beach, and the sea suddenly draws far back.

04:43.887 --> 04:45.982
What do you do? Here are the answers.

04:45.982 --> 04:49.264
In subduction the seafloor moves up and lifts the water.

04:49.264 --> 04:51.255
Sideways motion does not.

04:51.255 --> 04:54.263
A magnitude eight releases about a thousand times more energy

04:54.263 --> 04:58.250
than a six. And if the sea draws back, you go to high ground at

04:58.250 --> 04:59.888
once. Now go on to today's task.
